Lets begin by providing the rider’s account since we do not have access to Beehive. 

{quote}we have a case of a rider who wants invoice expenses to be listed from January 15th and February 22nd, 2024, totaling 28 sessions ( invoices). {quote}

Do I understand correctly they want all invoices for the rides taken by this rider during that period? 

{quote}Travessa das Águas Boas, Nº 27 /Pastelaria S. Carlos II, in S. Domingos de Benfica and the Foundation Champalimaud, in Belém, and return journey to the same address ( All 28 trips would be from the said address).{quote}

What does this mean? Do they want all the rides from this address and to this address? 

{quote}The *BOLT Transport Company's NIF* must also be included, with the purpose of delivering it to ADSE.{quote}

Why? What if they do not?

[~accountid:712020:b1a3aa39-56a5-492b-9453-5372da990388] Let me then: 

{quote}including getting the data{quote}

What data? and what if we do not know what to process? Do we take full responsibility?

{quote}Alternative: staging models could also be views, but then they have to be run in data pipelines. In this case they are available in DataHub. We can still expect ephemeral models, though.{quote}

I would lean towards the approach of not having ephemeral models in DataHub. These are internal entities within dbt projects, they shouldn’t even be exposed outside of a project. 

Of course, it’s up for discussion with the wider group of analytics engineers and analysts.

Hi [~accountid:712020:eb9cd4dd-46ca-47c8-a985-9f940495724a],

{quote}# Have both users been using one account since User A signed up using phone number that belonged to User B?{quote}

As far as I understand the situation, User B did not delete the account, when they gave back their number to their mobile phone operator. Then User A received the phone number from their mobile operator and was then able to log into the existing account. So only User A should be able to access the account, because we identify the account via the phone number to which only User A has access to. That’s at least the situation now.
Similar to the situation discussed in a previous case [https://taxify.atlassian.net/browse/LSS-33313|https://taxify.atlassian.net/browse/LSS-33313|smart-link] User B could have logged into their account, before we had logged them out of their account.



{quote}1.a. If yes, is there a possibility that User B could see the account details of User A, such as their name, saved addresses, contact details, date of birth, payment methods, etc.?{quote}

I have not asked yet Tech to check if something like had happened. It might have been possible, but based on the user’s report, it seems unlikely that User B could see the account details.

{quote}# Could you please confirm when was the date that User A created the account?{quote}

It looks like that the user created the account on the 24.06.2024.



{quote}# Is the debt for the scooter ride from 2023 the only financial loss User A incurred and are we going to reimburse User A for this loss?{quote}

Yes, it is the only loss. As far as I understand, we cannot refund any top-ups made to Bolt Balance. If needed, we could confirm it with Commerce.



{quote}# Were the trips User A complained about the only trips that User A got notified about and did not complete since they created the account?{quote}

They were also notified about a cash Ride-Hailing trip. Unfortunately, I cannot verify this, because I have only access to scooter related orders.

!image.jpg|width=3024,height=4032,alt="image.jpg"!

{quote}# From what I understood from the Beehive thread, both users were signed out from their accounts. Is there a possibility User A will keep receiving the log in attempts from User B until they delete the account and create a new one with a clean start?{quote}

Probably Infosec can answer this question better than me. But I believe that User A should keep receiving log in attempts until the account has been deleted and a new one created.

